<h2>How to estimate the real cost</h2><ol><li>Count the active agent seats that need full access.</li><li>Choose the lowest plan that includes your must-have ticketing, routing, SLA and reporting features.</li><li>Estimate AI Agent sessions and Copilot seats separately.</li><li>List connectors, MCP actions and other usage-based services that may be billed outside the seat price.</li><li>Check currency, taxes, billing cycle and any configuration loss if you downgrade.</li></ol>
<h2>Plan access and downgrade checks</h2><p>Before changing tiers, confirm which settings will remain available. The official Free Program guidance warns that moving from a higher tier can remove access to features and configurations that were part of the paid plan. Save your configuration notes, check whether any routing rules or reports depend on higher-tier features, and make a list of the items that need to be rebuilt if you later upgrade again. Do not assume a downgrade preserves every rule or reporting view.</p><h2>Example budget worksheet</h2><p>For illustration, five agents on Growth at US$19 per agent/month would mean US$95 per month on the displayed annual-billing rate, or US$1,140 across twelve months before tax and add-ons. Five agents on Pro at US$55 would be US$275 per month, or US$3,300 over twelve months. These calculations only multiply the published seat price; they do not include AI sessions beyond the allowance, Copilot, connectors, implementation work or local taxes. Use your own seat count and expected usage to build the estimate.</p>
