   'title'=>'How to Use Freshdesk: Setup and Ticket Workflow',
   'seo_title'=>'How to Use Freshdesk: Setup and Ticket Workflow',
   'description'=>'Set up Freshdesk email, tickets, assignments, portal content, automation and reports with a practical checklist for new support teams and avoid setup gaps.',
   'url'=>'/freshdesk/how-to-use/','date'=>'2026-10-29','status'=>'published',
   'content'=><<<'HTML'
<h2>Before you start</h2><p>Decide whether you need Freshdesk or Freshdesk Omni, which channels you will support, and who owns each type of request. Freshdesk is positioned as an email-first help desk; Omni is the separate multichannel product. Check the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/50000011778-understanding-freshdesk-pricing-plans">official plan guide</a> and <a href="https://www.freshworks.com/freshdesk/pricing/">pricing page</a> before setting up the team.</p>
<h2>1. Choose a plan for the workflow</h2><p>Growth, Pro and Enterprise have different routing, reporting and administration capabilities. The US pricing page checked October 29, 2026 lists US$19, US$55 and US$89 per agent/month billed annually. Confirm the price and feature table for your region, and check whether AI usage or connectors will add costs.</p>
<h2>2. Prepare the support inbox</h2><p>Choose the public support address, the people who will monitor it and the response rules customers should expect. Connect the address using the setup instructions in your Freshdesk account and send a test email from an external address. Verify that the ticket appears, the requester details are correct and the notification reaches the assigned team.</p>
<h2>3. Define ticket categories and priorities</h2><p>Start with a small set of categories that reflect real customer requests, such as billing, account access, product questions or technical issues. Set priority rules only where the team has a clear response process. Avoid creating many categories before agents understand when to use them.</p>
<h2>4. Set up assignment and team ownership</h2><p>Decide which tickets belong to each group and who reviews unassigned work. Use the available assignment controls and routing features in your plan. If you add automatic rules, document the conditions and fallback owner so a ticket cannot sit unnoticed when it does not match a rule.</p>
<h2>5. Create saved replies and internal notes</h2><p>Prepare concise replies for repeat questions, such as password reset instructions or a billing status request. Include the next step and the information the customer should provide. Keep internal notes separate from customer-facing messages, and assign someone to review templates when the product or policy changes.</p>
<h2>6. Build a useful customer portal and knowledge base</h2><p>Use the customer portal and knowledge base features included in your plan to answer common questions. Each article should solve one problem, use ordered steps and explain what to do if the steps fail. Link related help articles and review the content after a product change.</p>
<h2>7. Add automation carefully</h2><p>Start with low-risk rules such as assigning a ticket based on a clear category or sending an acknowledgement when a request arrives. Test each rule with examples that should match and examples that should not match. Keep an exception path for tickets that need a human decision.</p>
<h2>8. Review reports and service targets</h2><p>Use the reporting options in your plan to review incoming volume, overdue work, first response and resolution patterns. A fast first reply does not always mean a ticket was resolved quickly. If you use SLAs, define the target, owner and escalation step before relying on an alert.</p>
<h2>9. Check AI and add-on usage</h2><p>The pricing page lists the first 500 Freddy AI Agent sessions included once per account on Growth, Pro and Enterprise. Additional sessions, Copilot, connector tasks and MCP actions have separate charges. Estimate the volume and check the session definition before turning on automation across a large queue.</p>
<h2>10. Export and test before migrating data</h2><p>Freshdesk's filtered ticket export does not include the full conversation or archived ticket history. The official help center describes a separate account export for broader data. Read the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225158">ticket export guide</a>, <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/237405-how-can-i-export-tickets-including-the-content-of-the-ticket-">account export guide</a> and <a href="https://developers.freshdesk.com/api/">API documentation</a>. Export a small sample first and check comments, attachments, timestamps, requesters and custom fields.</p>
<h2>Test the full ticket lifecycle</h2><p>Use one sample request to follow the whole path from customer email to closure. Confirm the requester is recognized, the ticket lands in the intended group, the priority is correct, an agent can reply, the customer receives the reply and the ticket status changes as expected. Add a second sample that should go to a different group. This exposes routing gaps before the queue contains real customer requests. Keep the test messages clearly labeled so they are not confused with live support.</p>
<h2>Test automation rules with edge cases</h2><p>For each rule, write down the condition, expected action and fallback. Test a ticket that should match, one that almost matches and one that should not match. Check that two rules do not assign the same ticket to different groups or send duplicate acknowledgements. If the team uses SLA targets, verify what happens when a ticket is reopened or moved between groups. Keep automation simple until the category and ownership model is stable.</p>
<h2>Troubleshoot common setup issues</h2><ul><li><strong>Email does not create a ticket:</strong> verify the connected address, forwarding or verification steps in the account, then send a fresh external test message.</li><li><strong>Tickets land in the wrong group:</strong> review the category fields and rule order; remove overlapping conditions before adding another rule.</li><li><strong>Customers cannot find an answer:</strong> check that the knowledge article is published and uses the words customers search for, not only internal product names.</li><li><strong>Reports do not match expectations:</strong> check the date range, ticket status definitions and whether the required report is available in the selected plan.</li><li><strong>AI costs rise faster than expected:</strong> review session counts and the included allowance before expanding AI to more ticket types.</li></ul>
<h2>Review the first week</h2><p>After the team starts using Freshdesk, review unassigned tickets each day and collect examples of misrouted or reopened requests. At the end of the first week, compare incoming volume, first response, resolution time and the most common contact reasons with your baseline. Ask agents which fields or steps slow them down. Change one part of the workflow at a time so the team can see whether the change helps.</p>
<h2>Document who owns the setup</h2><p>Assign an owner for ticket categories, saved replies, knowledge articles, routing rules and reports. Set a review date for the rules and document the reason behind each important automation. Without a clear owner, a help desk can accumulate duplicate fields, outdated templates and rules that no one remembers how to maintain. Keep the setup guide with the team so a new administrator can understand the workflow.</p>
<h2>Launch checklist</h2><ul><li>Send a test request through every enabled channel.</li><li>Check ticket assignment, notifications, customer replies and internal notes.</li><li>Review saved replies and knowledge content with an agent.</li><li>Test automation rules with both matching and non-matching tickets.</li><li>Confirm reporting access, SLA rules and plan limits.</li><li>Document who owns the queue and how exceptions are escalated.</li></ul>
<h2>Related guides</h2><p>Start with the <a href="/freshdesk/">Freshdesk hub</a>, check the <a href="/freshdesk/pricing/">pricing guide</a>, read the <a href="/freshdesk/review/">review</a> and compare <a href="/freshdesk/alternatives/">alternatives</a>. For a direct competitor, see <a href="/compare/zendesk-vs-freshdesk/">Zendesk vs Freshdesk</a> and the <a href="/zendesk/how-to-use/">Zendesk setup guide</a>.</p>
